culture trafficking illegal excavations  and the antiquities trade in the  interview the president of the Prussian  Cultural Heritage Foundation Haman part  singer mr. part singer  why should museums today be extremely  careful when dealing with ancient  antiquities one davison does should we  know that illegal excavations and grave  robbing are an enormous worldwide  problem not just in countries that are  in turmoil but even in Germany where  there continues to be an illegal trade  in antiquities it's worth billions in  trade each year that's why I believe the  question of provenance of legal  provenance and the legal trade in  antiquities is of central importance for  museums criminologists always talk about  the trade in illegal antiquities in the  same breath as the weapons and illegal  drug trades are there estimates as to  how big the global trade in illegal  antiquities is it's true that they're  often linked together I don't know if  that's absolutely the case but the fact  is that it is an international trade  it's not a question of whether the trade  is worth 1 million 1 billion or 3  billion a year but the addiction to  profit is a real threat to humanity's  cultural heritage and we have to rein it  in you're an archeologist yourself  you've worked in Turkey Russia and  Central Asia did you come in contact  with grave robbers there it's been  finding I did come into contact with the  damage the grave robbers caused you find  robbed graves in almost all of those  countries you see that especially when  the burial site is above ground as a  grave mound or if it's covered by rocks  you know where to look in those cases  you see that frequently and sometimes  you come up against the grave robbers as  I did in northern Pakistan it was there  that I ended up looking down the barrel  of a Kalashnikov we came by chance upon  grave robbers and we surprised them as  they were robbing a stupa a holy  Buddhist structure that contained relics  which they were removing  good funding Taliban it's known that the  Taliban partly financed their operations  in Afghanistan by grave robbing the same  is true of al Qaeda in Iraq what role do  stolen antiquities play today in  financing international terrorism and in  Islamist terrorism we know that members  of is of Islamic state have engaged in  grave robbing and that they have sold  antiquities illegally I can't say how  big a source of income that is however  but it remains a fact that the damage to  cultural heritage is immeasurable that  doesn't just apply to cultural artifacts  that have been removed through illegal  excavations and sold it also applies to  the case of the Buddha statues of  Bamiyan or the mosque in Mosul that was  destroyed by Islamic state just a few  weeks ago or the Islamist group in  Timbuktu in Mali who a few months ago  destroyed holy Islamic graves we're  seeing the loss and destruction of  Islamic culture being pursued with total  zeal and that is something that is  simply unspeakable what does that look  like in reality mrs. Kuan ayahs group in  Syria discovers something how do they  get those artifacts onto the market no  indeed it's relatively simple to remove  objects from the ground in Syria or in  Iraq they identify places of antiquity  they can identify mud brick structures  that have deformed over the ages you  don't need the eye of an archaeologist  to do that those places are relatively  well known and there's also plenty of  literature available as well as tourist  guide books they simply take a bulldozer  or metal detector and they try to find  evidence of important archaeological  items then they tear them from the  ground they deal in objects that the  grave robbers can quickly turn into as  much money as possible works of art clay  tablets and similar items they're less  interested in textiles which are  extremely important to archaeologists  then those objects end up in illegal  channels that have international  connects  the question is who's making the largest  amount of money at which point the  international trade in illegal  antiquities has meeting points around  the world no matter where antiquities  come from whether it's Syria Latin  America Africa or from Germany they're  being traded internationally what's the  best way for the authorities to combat  the international network of dealers  Sinise mom is naturally designed for  first of all it's mainly a problem of  crisis countries where the state  structures and authorities have to be  reestablished there needs to be an  infrastructure and the public needs to  be made aware of what's happening when  villagers who live near a place of  antiquity see grave robbers they must be  made aware that they're destroying our  past our history and they have to take  action by informing the police at the  very least if they see that happening  that applies to people in Germany as  much as it does to people in Iraq the  second thing we need are effective laws  in the countries where we know that  there is illegal trade in antiquities  that's why the reform of Germany's  cultural treasure law which politicians  are planning is of such immense  importance that will limit Germany as a  focal point for the illegal trade let's  talk about Germany a little more for a  moment  almost every court case involving  illegal trade in antiquities has been  dropped why is that the case why is it  that the illegal trade in antiquities is  treated as a minor offence how do you  explain that yes it's true that that is  a widely held belief but things are  changing when it comes to the public's  perception such as when someone visits  Turkey as a tourist or any other country  and they're offered antiques to buy as  souvenirs people need to be clear that  that is not only illegal but by buying  antiquities they're encouraging the  destruction of cultural heritage the  United Nations and UNESCO have an  organization dedicated to protecting  cultural artifacts how are they dealing  with the problem of stolen antiquities  and the trade in illegal goods  Guinan t1 t1 s competition for union  many years ago in 1970 UNESCO agreed a  convention that is extremely important  for this subject it's aimed at limiting  the illegal trade in stolen antiquities  but that convention can only be  effective when it's integrated into  national law Germany did that in 2008  however according to the law cultural  artifacts are only protected when  they're specifically listed that has  made legal disputes very complicated we  have to acknowledge two things at this  point first not every museum in the  world has a full list of cultural  artifacts and has published it  internationally that's one point the  main area of concern is illegal  excavation objects that are still in the  ground and which thieves remove from the  earth those things cannot be listed  that's a crucial issue the reform of the  law dictates that you have to have an  official document proving an artifacts  origin you also need to have an official  document approving export from the  country of origin and not just from a  third country there's a proposal that  could have very big consequences the  suggestion is to ban the global trade in  antiquities completely that would solve  the problem in one swoop because the  markers would dry off what do you think  of that yes that would be a radical  solution but there is trade in  antiquities which is legal there are  many objects that have been in  collections since the 17th 18th and 19th  centuries why should they not be traded  further I don't think there's a problem  with that I think the simplest thing to  do is to adapt the relevant laws so that  they can be effective and that they're  put into practice in reality there's  another point where laws don't help at  all the police and customs authorities  need to be able to recognize the illegal  antiquities recently we published in  Germany the red list of objects from  Syria drawn up by the International  Council of museums that list has  categories and groups of antiquities  that are characteristic for cultures  that existed in Syrian territory  if such objects turn out then the  authorities the customs authorities will  immediately be able to recognize that  they could have their origins in Syria  and then they can take action you've  mentioned collections in western museums  every so often there is quite passionate  debate about returning certain historic  objects to their country of origin here  in Berlin the Nefertiti bust which Egypt  has made repeated claims on is often  mentioned Germany says that claim is not  legitimate can you briefly explain the  situation as it stands now it's  important to say immediately that the  Egyptian government has never made an  official demand for the return of the  Nefertiti bust those claims were made by  individuals by former representatives of  the antiquities authorities or by the  media but there's no official request  for the return because it's known that  at the time there was a law defining how  antiquities were to be shared out when a  foreign archaeological team carried out  a dig with the permission of the  Egyptians the discovered artifacts would  be divided up as compensation for the  expenses the team had paid out the same  was true in Turkey that system applied  to the finding of Nefertiti at the time  the Egyptian authorities had to receive  a complete list and photographs of the  most important pieces before dividing up  the find the fairy tale that the  Nefertiti bust was in a box covered with  clay and that the Egyptians knew nothing  about it is completely false at the time  the Egyptians were more interested in  another artifact in the find we have to  recognize that the hype surrounding  Nefertiti only came later in short the  important thing is whether these objects  were legally claimed at the time of the  find that's the guiding concept for us  every museum needs to be ready to  discuss returning an object to its  rightful owners if in the course of  researching its provenance it's found  that it was illegally obtained that  applies not only to antiquity stolen by  the Nazis but to all cultural artifacts  z32 you can--this begin right at the  very beginning you mentioned stolen  artifacts in Germany grave robbing and  its associated commercial aspect is  something that had  more often in the near and Middle East  in Germany it's about treasure hunters  with metal detectors how much damage are  they causing director Norman charred  nitrogen of course they're causing  enormous damage just imagine that the  most important archaeological find of  Europe's prehistoric era the bronze  Nebra sky disk was found illegally a few  years ago the disk is the oldest known  representation of the cosmos it's an  astonishing work of abstraction it was  here in what is now Germany that our  ancestors turned the observation of the  heavens into a two-dimensional object  long before the ancient Egyptians that's  amazing if that object had disappeared  down an illegal Channel we wouldn't know  anything about it today in decent  Canadian for soybean it's the for my  part senior president of the Prussian  cultural heritage foundation thank you  for giving you insights to Deutsche  Welle
 

